<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catherine of Navarre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1468">1468</a>–<a href="/years/1517">1517</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> (1468–1517), also called Catherine of Foix, was queen of Navarre from 1483 until her death. She ruled jointly with her husband John III of Albret, and their reign was troubled by the rival ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1512 Ferdinand the Catholic accused them of siding with France and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-navarre">Upper Navarre</a>, leaving the couple only the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catherine-of-navarre.jpg" alt="Catherine of Navarre" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Lacroix · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catherine_de_Foix.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Catherine was the younger daughter of Gaston of Foix, Prince of Viana, and Magdalena of Valois, the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a>. She was born during the reign of her great-grandfather King John II, who was succeeded in 1479 by her grandmother Eleanor. Their father had already died, so when Eleanor died the same year the crown passed to Catherine's brother Francis Phoebus. She was also Duchess of Gandia, Montblanc and Peñafiel, Countess of Foix, Bigorre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, and Viscountess of Béarn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The death of Francis in 1483 made Catherine queen under the regency of their mother. Her uncle John of Foix claimed the throne, appealing to the Salic Law, and began a civil war (1483–1492) that revived the old conflict of the Beaumont and Agramont parties. In 1484 Magdalena decided to marry the 15-year-old Catherine to John of Albret, from a noble family of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. The wedding took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lescar">Lescar</a> in 1486, but the coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> was deferred until 1494. When Aragon took Magdalena hostage in 1494, Catherine took control of the government herself, at twenty-six.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> had allied with the Pope against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and presented claims to the royal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Catherine did not accept them, and Ferdinand sent Fadrique Álvarez de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, 2nd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, who occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> on 25 July 1512.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Catherine and John III fled to Béarn and based themselves at Pau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthez">Orthez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbes">Tarbes</a>. On 23 March 1513 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, reduced to the pro-Spanish Beaumont party, pledged allegiance to Ferdinand, and in 1515 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-navarre">Upper Navarre</a> was annexed to the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. In 1516 John III and Pedro, Marshal of Navarre, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> to reconquer the kingdom but failed to reach its heartland. John died on 17 June 1516, and Catherine died at Mont-de-Marsan on 12 February 1517. From 1512 she was queen in fact only in parts of Lower Navarre, north of the Pyrenees. She had thirteen children, and her son Henry II of Navarre was born in 1503.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Catherine of Navarre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1512, during the Italian Wars, Ferdinand the Catholic accused the Albret monarchs, John III and <b>Catherine of Foix</b>, of siding with France and sent the Duke of Alba to seize Pamplona.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Mont-de-Marsan, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.89,-0.5&amp;year=1499" aria-label="Mont-de-Marsan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4538 609 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Catherine of Navarre"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4988 879h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4988 879h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=43.89,-0.5&amp;year=1499">What was here in 1499? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
